so for the reasons given in the  judgments we have today handed down  this appeal  will be dismissed  and what i propose to do now is to read  out a  summary of the judgment a short summary  so as to explain to the press and to the  public  what we have decided  it should be noted however that this  summary forms no part of our decision  but it's just provided for information  and the better understanding of the  decision  the the central question  in the case  was whether the judge then mr justice  warby now  lord justice warby had been right  to grant summary judgment for breach of  privacy  and breach of copyright  to megan duchess of sussex  against the publishers of the mail on  sunday newspaper and mail online  associated newspapers limited  the claims related to the publication in  the mail on sunday and the mail online  i'll call them together mail on sunday  of about half the contents of a  five-page  hand-written letter which i'll call the  letter  which the duchess had sent on the 27th  of august 2018 to her father  mr thomas markle  the court of appeal  upheld the judge's decision  that the duchess had a reasonable  expectation of privacy in the contents  of the letter  and those contents were personal  private  and not matters of legitimate public  interest  the articles in the mail on sunday  interfered with the duchess's reasonable  expectation of privacy  and were not a justified or  proportionate means of correcting  inaccuracies about the letter  that had been contained in an article  published on the 6th of february  2019  in people magazine in the united states  the key point  was that the mail on sunday articles  focused on revealing the contents of the  letter  rather than providing mr markle's  response  to the attack on him  in the people in people magazine  the headline  was revealed  the letter showing  true tragedy of megan's rift with a  father she says has broken her heart  into a million pieces  and the first line of the mail on sunday  article read  the full content of a sensational letter  written by the duchess to her estranged  father  shortly after her wedding  can be revealed for the first time today  those passages demonstrated that the  mail on sunday articles were splashed  as a new public revelation  of extracts from the dutchesses letter  to her father  rather than her father's answers to what  people magazine had written  associated newspapers argued that the  judge had failed  to realize how people magazine had  produced mr markle  by alleging that he had cold-shouldered  his daughter at the time of the wedding  lied about her shutting him out  and ignored her pleas for reconciliation  in a loving letter  the court of appeal found  that the judge had been right to decide  that just one paragraph of the letter  could have been justifiably deployed  to rebut the allegation in people  magazine that the duchess's letter was  loving  when in fact it was not it was a letter  reprimanding mr markle for talking to  the press  and asking him to stop doing so  the court of appeal rejected associated  newspapers arguments that the judge had  firstly applied the wrong legal test  in deciding where the balance lay  between the duchesses article 8 writes  in  the letter  and the rights of mr markle and  associated newspapers to freedom of  speech under article 10.  also the court of appeal rejected  associated newspapers arguments  that the judge had failed properly to  evaluate  associated newspapers defenses to the  duchess's breach of copyright claim  first fair dealing in reporting current  events  and secondly the public interest in  reporting the letter  it had been common ground that the judge  had correctly stated the legal  principles that were applicable  to the question of whether summary  judgment should be granted  the court of appeal commented that the  new evidence that had been provided to  it  had also been widely publicized in the  press nationally and internationally  and in those circumstances the court  decided that the new evidence should be  admitted  as a matter of pure pragmatism  even though  it was more directed to the drafting of  the letter  and to what the duchess knew about the  contacts between  the kensington palace communications  team  and the authors of a book finding  freedom  than to the issues in the appeal  despite actually finding the evidence  the new evidence of little assistance  the court of appeal noted that the  duchess had apologized to the court  for the pleading served on her behalf  that said she did not know if and to  what extent  the communications team were involved in  providing information for the book  it it had appeared from new evidence  from mr canal  that he had provided some information to  the authors of the book  with the duchess's knowledge  the court of appeals said that this was  at best  an unfortunate lapse of memory on her  part but did not bear on the issues and  had been given no prominence in  associated newspapers oral argument  so for the detailed reasons given in my  judgment  the judge's careful decision mostly on  factual questions was upheld  and it was hard to see what evidence  could have been induced at trial that  would have altered that situation  the judge had been  we found in as good a position  as any trial judge  to look at the article in people  magazine  the letter itself and the mail on sunday  articles  to decide if publication of the contents  of the letter  was appropriate to rebut the allegations  made against mr markle  the judge had correctly decided  that whilst it might have been  proportionate to publish a very small  part of the letter for that purpose it  was not necessary to publish half the  contents  of the five-page letter as associated  newspapers had done  the court reiterated in conclusion  the narrowness of the issues  that it had had to decide  so that concludes the summary  um all consequential matters will be  dealt with in writing  um after today in the usual way  uh unless council have any submissions  they'd like to make about that  um that concludes this short hearing  no submissions from me milo or for me  thank you mr caldwell  all right  you
 

